body:prose = >`
  &ND v2

  # Appendix — Canonical AEON Form [# appendix-canonical-aeon-form]

  [* Appendix to:] AEON Specification v1


  ## Overview [# overview]

  Canonical AEON defines a deterministic textual representation suitable for hashing, diffing, signing, and reproducible builds.

  Canonical form is [* optional] for parsing but REQUIRED for canonical emitters.


  ## Document Structure [# document-structure]

  ### Header [# header]

  If no header exists, emit the default:

  ~~~$ aeon
  aeon:header = {
    encoding = "utf-8"
    mode = "transport"
    profile = "core"
    version = "1.0"
  }
  ~~~$

  Header fields use the reference-safe key ordering defined below. The header remains before every body binding; sorting MUST NOT move a binding across the header/body boundary.

  ### Top-Level Order [# top-level-order]

  1. [$ aeon:header] first
  2. All body bindings in reference-safe key order

  This ordering is not formatter-only advice:

  - a structured header appearing after any body binding is invalid input;
  - canonicalization MUST reject such input rather than reordering it into a valid document.


  ## Keys and Objects [# keys-and-objects]

  - Object keys use reference-safe key order
  - Attribute keys use reference-safe key order
  - One key per line in multi-line objects

  Canonical layout is structure-sensitive:

  - inline object forms may remain inline when the enclosing canonical layout is inline;
  - when an enclosing object or list is rendered as multiline canonical layout, nested object values also canonically expand to multiline object blocks rather than remaining inline;
  - key sorting still applies at every level regardless of whether the object is rendered inline or multiline.

  ### Reference Safe Key Ordering [# reference-safe-key-ordering]

  At each sortable scope, emit the smallest decoded key whose local predecessor entries have already been emitted. Local predecessors are obtained by projecting Core reference dependencies into that scope as described below. Targets already available on entry to the scope are satisfied dependencies, not additional local predecessor entries. Reconsider the eligible keys after every emission. This is dependency ordering with a lexical tie-break, not a pairwise key comparison that sometimes ignores a reference.

  Sortable scopes are the body root, header fields, every object's members, and every attribute head's entries. This includes objects and attributes nested inside lists, tuples, node children, or other metadata. Lists, tuples, and node children retain their positions and never become sortable scopes.

  Lexical key comparison uses the decoded sequence of Unicode scalar values, not locale collation, encoded escape spelling, or UTF-16 code units. Compare at the first differing scalar value; if one sequence is a prefix of another, the shorter key comes first. Do not normalize case or Unicode composition. Reference-free scopes therefore remain lexically sorted under this comparator.

  Reference dependencies come only from actual clone and pointer occurrences. A reference inside a descendant value or node-head attribute can constrain the order of its containing binding. Reference-shaped strings, symbols, separator payloads, and SANSA literals do not create dependencies.

  For every reference, use Core's exact decoded target and availability rules. At each sortable scope containing both occurrences in distinct entry subtrees, the entry containing the target's availability point precedes the entry containing the reference. Apply this constraint to containing scopes as well as directly referenced members. Do not infer containment by testing textual path prefixes.

  Within a nested scope, a reference to an external target already made available by an enclosing scope does not wait for an entry in the nested scope. An unrelated local entry with the same decoded key as an external target is not its predecessor. If an external target is not yet available, its dependency must be enforced at the containing sortable scope that orders the target and reference subtrees; it must not be dropped merely because it is external to the nested scope.

  A container's availability point precedes its children; it is not the end of its subtree. An ancestor-container target therefore does not make a child depend on its own enclosing entry. Attached metadata precedes its payload, so a payload may reference its already-declared metadata without acquiring an artificial cycle. Binding/key attributes and implicit node-head attributes remain distinct; sorting does not add a source-path spelling for an implicit node head.

  For example, this valid authored order:

  ~~~$ aeon
  d = 3
  c = 2
  b = ~c
  a = 1
  ~~~$

  has this canonical body order:

  ~~~$ aeon
  a = 1
  c = 2
  b = ~c
  d = 3
  ~~~$

  After emitting [$ c], [$ b] becomes eligible and precedes the still-eligible [$ d]. The same rule applies within attribute heads: [$ a@{z = 1, b = ~a.@.z} = 0] retains [$ z] before [$ b], rather than introducing a forward reference by sorting [$ b] first.

  Canonicalization MUST validate references in the original authored order before adding a default header or sorting any scope. Missing, forward, and self references remain errors even if reordering or default-header insertion could make them valid. This is Core reference validation, not reference evaluation, profile approval, or schema validation.

  Emitters MUST preserve reference kind, exact decoded target, datatype, structural identity, source plane, and indexed positions. The result MUST remain legal under the same Core reference rules. If validation fails or ordering cannot satisfy the constraints, return a diagnostic and no successful or partial canonical text. Resource-limit failure must not fall back to alphabetical or authored-order output presented as canonical success.

  Successful canonicalization satisfies three ordering invariants:

  - legality: the emitted document passes the same Core reference validation;
  - idempotence: canonicalizing the emitted canonical text produces the same canonical text;
  - minimality: at every emission step, the selected entry has the smallest decoded key among the currently eligible entries. A legal but non-minimal topological ordering is not canonical.

  ### Ordering Compatibility [# ordering-compatibility]

  Reference-safe ordering changes canonical bytes where lexical sorting alone would violate reference legality. The explicit scalar-value comparator can also change ordering in implementations that previously compared UTF-16 code units. Hashes, signatures, caches, and reproducible fixtures must use an explicitly identified canonical contract; historical released CTS snapshots retain their original expectations and are not rewritten to adopt this ordering.


  ## Lists [# lists]

  - Element order preserved (never reordered)
  - Single-line for simple scalars, multi-line for complex values

  When a list is rendered multiline, complex object elements canonically expand to multiline object blocks.


  ## Scalars [# scalars]

  ### Numbers [# numbers]

  - Canonicalization of [$ :n] values is value-normalizing while preserving the
    broad representation family chosen by the author:
    - integer family
    - decimal family
    - exponent family

  - Remove all [$ _] separators
  - No leading [$ +] sign
  - Leading-dot decimals gain an explicit zero ([$ .5] → [$ 0.5])
  - Decimal-family values trim redundant trailing fractional zeroes, but retain at
    least one fractional digit ([$ 10.00] → [$ 10.0])

  - Exponent-family values use lowercase [$ e]
  - Exponent-family values remove redundant exponent sign and leading exponent
    zeroes ([$ 1.0E+03] → [$ 1e3])

  - Zero follows the same family model rather than a special ad hoc rule:
    - integer zero → [$ 0] or [$ -0]
    - decimal zero → [$ 0.0] or [$ -0.0]
    - exponent zero → [$ 0e0] or [$ -0e0]

  ~~~$ aeon
  // Non-canonical → Canonical
  1_000_000   → 1000000
  1.2300      → 1.23
  .10         → 0.10
  .5          → 0.5
  1.0E+03     → 1e3
  10.00       → 10.0
  0.0e+0      → 0e0
  ~~~$

  ### Radix [# radix]

  - Canonicalization of [$ :radix\\[...\\]] values remains representation-preserving
    rather than value-normalizing

  - [$ _] separators are removed from the canonical payload
  - the remaining digit sequence, decimal point placement, and leading zero width
    are otherwise preserved

  ~~~$ aeon
  // Non-canonical → Canonical
  %10_00      → %1000
  %10.00      → %10.00
  %0010.00    → %0010.00
  ~~~$

  ### Booleans [# booleans]

  Always [$ true] or [$ false] (lowercase).

  ### Toggle [# toggle]

  Preserve original literal ([$ yes], [$ on], etc.).

  ### Base Literals [# base-literals]

  - Hex: lowercase ([$ #ff00aa])
  - Remove [$ _] separators

  ### Strings [# strings]

  - Always double quotes ([$ "])
  - Minimal escaping
  - Raw line breaks → [$ \\\\n]
  - Non-ASCII preserved as UTF-8

  Canonical strings preserve their literal family:

  - ordinary strings always emit as escaped double-quoted strings, including when their decoded value contains line breaks
  - trimticks always retain the [$ >] marker and backtick delimiters
  - canonical output never uses tabs in a multiline trimtick gutter
  - canonical equality is determined by the literal family and the resulting decoded or trimmed string value
  - one-line trimticks use the compact trimtick form, including inside inline containers
  - multiline trimticks rendered inside inline object or attribute forms remain compact trimticks with escaped line breaks rather than collapsing to ordinary strings

  ### Separator Literals [# separator-literals]

  Canonical separator literals:

  - No whitespace between [$ =] and [$ ^]
  - No raw whitespace outside quoted segments
  - Raw segments are emitted verbatim
  - Quoted segments use canonical quoted-string escaping

  ~~~$ aeon
  // Non-canonical → Canonical
  size:sep["x"] =^300x250   → size:sep["x"] = ^300x250
  ~~~$

  Quoted segments preserve their string content:

  ~~~$ aeon
  data:sep["|"] = ^"one "|" two"   // preserved as-is
  ~~~$

  ### Symbolic Literals [# symbolic-literals]

  Canonical symbolic literals use pipe delimiters and the decoded payload. A literal pipe is escaped as [$ \\|], backslash and control characters use the ordinary canonical string escapes, and quote characters remain unescaped:

  ~~~$ aeon
  stage:symbol = |in review|
  message:symbol = |say "yes" and \\|wait\\||
  ~~~$


  ## References [# references]

  References MUST preserve semantic intent:

  - clone references remain clone references ([$ ~...])
  - pointer references remain pointer references ([$ ~>...])

  Canonical form MUST NOT:

  - Change a clone-intent reference into a pointer-intent reference or vice versa
  - Inline or resolve references
  - Alter the logical value graph

  Canonical reference rendering also applies these normalizations:

  - explicit root prefixes are elided when redundant ([$ ~$.a] → [$ ~a], [$ ~>$.a] → [$ ~>a])
  - quoted member or attribute selectors may collapse to bare identifier form when the decoded segment is already a canonical bare identifier ([$ ~a.@.\\["meta"\\]] [>] [$ ~a.@.meta])

  ## Node Heads [# node-heads]

  Canonical node-head ordering is:

  - [$ tag@{...}:datatype]

  Canonical form preserves this order for node heads in the same way it preserves [$ key@{...}:type] ordering for bindings.


  ## Whitespace [# whitespace]

  - 2-space indentation (no tabs)
  - One space around [$ =]
  - LF line endings ([$ \\\\n])
  - Opening brace on same line as binding
  - Closing brace on own line

  ~~~$ aeon
  user = {
    name = "Patrik"
    age = 49
  }
  ~~~$


  ## Non-Goals [# non-goals]

  Canonical form does NOT:

  - Alter the logical value graph
  - Inline references
  - Change types
  - Add or remove bindings (except default header)
`
